{"name":"Classic Chalk","hex":"#f4f4f0","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"classic","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Of or relating to the first class or rank, especially in literature or art.","Exemplary of a particular style; defining a class or category; typical; archetypical; epitomic."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A perfect and/or early example of a particular style.","An artistic work of lasting worth, such as a film or song; a work of enduring excellence."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/classic"},{"term":"chalk","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A soft, white, powdery limestone (calcium carbonate, CaCO3).","A piece of chalk, or nowadays processed compressed gypsum (calcium sulfate, CaSO4), that is used for drawing and for writing on a blackboard (chalkboard)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To apply chalk to anything, such as the tip of a billiard cue.","To record something, as on a blackboard, using chalk."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/chalk"}],"entries":[{"title":"Classic","blocks":[{"text":"A classic is an outstanding example of a particular style; something of lasting worth or with a timeless quality; of the first or highest quality, class, or rank – something that exemplifies its class. The word can be an adjective or a noun. It denotes a particular quality in art, architecture, literature, design, technology, or other cultural artifacts. In commerce, products are named 'classic' to denote a long-standing popular version or model, to distinguish it from a newer variety. Classic is used to describe many major, long-standing sporting events. Colloquially, an everyday occurrence may be described in some dialects of English as 'an absolute classic'."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Classic"},{"title":"Chalk","blocks":[{"text":"Chalk is a soft, white, porous, sedimentary carbonate rock. It is a form of limestone composed of the mineral calcite and originally formed under the sea by the accumulation and lithification of hard parts of organisms, mostly microscopic plankton, which had settled to the sea floor. Chalk is common throughout Western Europe, where deposits underlie parts of France, and steep cliffs are often seen where they meet the sea in places such as the Dover cliffs on the Kent coast of the English Channel."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2f/Seven_Sisters_3.jpg/330px-Seven_Sisters_3.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chalk"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg/500px-Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/17/Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg/500px-Nitzana_chalk_curves_%282%29%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Nitzana Chalk curves\" situated at Western Negev, Israel, are chalk deposits formed in the Mesozoic era's Tethys Ocean","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANitzana_chalk_curves_(2)%2C_Western_Negev%2C_Israel.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG/500px-Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG/1280px-Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/91/Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG/500px-Seale_Chalk_Pit_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Open chalk pit, Seale, Surrey, UK","link":"https://en.